* Mon Sep 28 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.2.0-1
- New `chain-save` verb: write a filter chain's live control values into the
  conf.d drop-in that declares it, so an EQ curve survives the chain stopping.
  Nothing else persists one. Measured 2026-09-28: WirePlumber's entire state
  is default-nodes, default-routes and stream-properties, none of which has
  any model for a filter control port -- so before this a reboot brought every
  band back flat, as one did.
- It does NOT restart anything, which is what makes it safe. The running chain
  is already in the state being written, so the file only matters at the next
  start; restarting filter-chain.service would destroy and recreate the sink,
  and on this host that sink is the default, so every playing stream would be
  bumped off it mid-save.
- New `config` capability, default NO, and deliberately not folded into
  `params`. Moving a slider is audible, reversible and confined to the running
  graph; writing a file the audio server executes at every start afterwards is
  none of those, and it is the only verb here that reaches outside the graph
  to the filesystem.
- The edit is textual and surgical rather than parse-and-reserialise.
  spa-json-dump would give a clean tree and writing it back would be valid
  SPA-JSON -- and would drop every comment in the file. These drop-ins are
  documentation as much as configuration, so only the bytes between one pair
  of braces move, and the result is re-parsed with spa-json-dump before it
  replaces anything. Previous contents go to .bak; a filter the file does not
  name, or names without a control block, is reported in `skipped` rather than
  silently dropped.
- chain-save trusts the daemon's own writes over the graph, and that is not
  belt-and-braces. Re-measured 2026-09-28 against a chain recreated by a
  PipeWire restart: three node-param writes stayed invisible in pw-dump and
  then appeared exactly as written the moment the chain was instantiated. A
  first cut of this verb read the graph, saved the values the user was trying
  to replace, and reported success doing it. Every node-param is now
  remembered until the graph echoes it, and the reply names those keys in
  `pending`. The record is dropped on `reset`, because ids are reused.

* Sun Sep 27 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.0-1
- Project a filter graph's control ports, and add node-param to move them. A
  filter-chain node carries TWO Props entries and the projection read only the
  first, which is audioconvert's -- so volume and mute worked and an EQ's
  Freq/Q/Gain were invisible. The second entry is keyed <filter-node>:<port>
  and is now carried as `controls`, with the biquad coefficients flagged
  read-only for the UI's benefit (docs/56).
- New `params` capability, default yes. It is the same kind of thing as
  `mixer`: audible, reversible, and pointless to gate if the point is an
  effects UI that can move a slider. It creates nothing -- a chain has to exist
  first, which still means `nodes` + `modules` or a hand-written conf.d file.
- node-param will only set a control the node ALREADY advertises. That is a
  security boundary and not tidiness: the audioconvert Props entry shares the
  same param id and carries `debug.wav-path`, which writes the node's audio to
  a file. Filter control names always carry a colon and audioconvert's never
  do, which is the line the verb refuses to cross -- the concern
  `links.capture` exists for, arriving through a different door.
- The adjustable EQ is chained bq_* nodes, not param_eq. Measured 2026-09-28:
  param_eq and libpipewire-module-parametric-equalizer publish no control ports
  at all, so their curves are config-time and no slider can reach them. A
  chained graph that omits filter.graph's inputs/outputs comes out stereo with
  one shared set of controls, which is exactly what a per-band slider wants.
- The docs shipped here now cover the app's Effects screen, which is where the
  `controls` above surface: one slider per adjustable control, the biquad
  coefficients dropped rather than shown, and slider ranges keyed on the PORT
  name because the filter's LABEL never reaches the graph -- the filter node's
  name is whatever the config author chose. A port with no known range gets a
  number field rather than a slider with invented ends. The app itself is
  still not packaged here; pw-app/build.sh --install remains the delivery.
- No protocol version bump. The addition is additive, and the policy map is
  already the capability advertisement the app reads: a daemon older than this
  has no `params` key in it at all, so its absence distinguishes "too old to
  have controls" from "this node has no filter graph", which the absent
  `controls` field alone could not.

* Fri Sep 25 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.0-1
- First package. Host-side PipeWire graph control for Waydroid's Android:
  nodes, ports, links, volumes, defaults, device profiles and routes, and the
  graph quantum (docs/55).
- Needs no PipeWire bindings: `pw-dump --monitor` is already a delta protocol,
  so the graph mirror is a subprocess and a line loop rather than a libpipewire
  client vendored against whatever the host runs.
- Runs unprivileged as a --user unit, because PipeWire is a user service. Only
  the profile publisher is root, and only because the Android app's private
  directory is mode 0700 owned by the app's uid.
- Capability policy ships default-deny for links whose source is a capture
  device or a monitor port, for node creation, and for module hosting.
- The Android half is deliberately not packaged, for the reason no app here is:
  an APK needs the Android SDK and the Kotlin compiler at build time, and a
  prebuilt one would be the vendored binary docs/54 rules out.
